  "query_preview": "..\n(This afternoon through Wednesday)\nIssued at 1117 AM CST THU NOV 5 2026\n\nThe main frontal boundary is slowly moving out of Central AL\ntoday with only isolated showers remaining across the far\nsoutheastern counties as we go through the day. Skies will be\nclearing as the drier air slowly filters into the region. The\nexception may come late tonight into early tomorrow morning when\npatchy dense fog may develop again, mainly near rivers or low-\nlying valleys that saw a lot of rainfall yesterday.  A secondary\ntrough is expected to swing through on Friday bringing a\nreinforcing shot of drier air as we go into the weekend.\nThe main story here will be some of the coldest temperatures\nwe've seen so far this Fall across much of the area. Overnight\nlows Saturday into Sunday morning are expected to drop into the\nlow to mid 30s for most of the area, leading to the potential for\nfrost and freeze conditions. Right now, there's at least a 70-75%\nchance for frost development across much of the northern counties\nand through the Black Belt region. The probability of freezing\nconditions in those areas drops to around 20-30%.\n\nSurface high pressure will build in over the weekend with this\ncooler air and remain in place through at least Monday. From\nthere, a cut-off upper low will slowly slide through Texas and\ninto the Gulf States through the middle part of next week. Some\nguidance tries to develop some rainfall with this, but moisture\nreturn ahead of this system looks minimal at best, so we'll hold\noff on including rain in the forecast through mid-week.\n\n25/Owen",
